{"title":"Algebraically closed","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>In <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Abstract_algebra\" title=\"Abstract algebra\">abstract algebra</a>, a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Field\" title=\"Field\">field</a> is said to be <b>algebraically closed</b> if any nonconstant <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial\" title=\"Polynomial\">polynomial</a> with <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Coefficient\" title=\"Coefficient\">coefficients</a> in the field also has a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Root\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Root\">root</a> in the field. The field of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complex_number\" title=\"Complex number\">complex numbers</a>, denoted <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/8/568df917149216103d9ba89ab22426e9449fb883.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{C}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" />, is a well-known example of an algebraically closed field. \n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Algebraically_closed&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017083040\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.007 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.011 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 10/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 301/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 179/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.447      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.447      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:3760:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017083040 and revision id 